    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d251">

                <pb facs="0309" n="295" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg295"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.50/12–2044</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Adviser on Far Eastern Investment and
                        Finance, Division of Financial and Monetary Affairs</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Remer</hi>)<anchor n="50" xml:id="d251-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1944-12-20">December 20, 1944</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>You will find attached a summary statement on the interest of the State
                    Department in Donald Nelson’s work in China and the following Supplements: <list>
                        <label>1.</label>
                        <item>The Important Documents.<anchor n="51" xml:id="d251-f2"/>
                        </item>
                        <label>2.</label>
                        <item>American Assistance to the Chinese in the Planning of
                            Industrialization and Economic Development.</item>
                        <label>3.</label>
                        <item>Mr. Nelson’s Proposed Committee.<anchor n="51" xml:id="d251-f3"/>
                        </item>
                        <label>4.</label>
                        <item>The Yangtze Gorge Project.<anchor n="51" xml:id="d251-f4"/>
                        </item>
                    </list>
                </p>
                <p>This summary statement and its supporting documents have been prepared in the
                    hope that they may be of assistance in the unification of the work on American
                    economic relations with China. Special attention has been given to long run
                    economic development and related problems. This work will no doubt grow in
                    volume and importance during the coming months and over the next few years. We
                    should welcome Mr. Nelson’s participation and try to bring it into proper
                    relation with the economic work now going forward in the Department.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">C. F. Remer</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>




                <xi:include href="d251-a1.xml" xpointer="d251-a1"/>
                <xi:include href="d251-a2.xml" xpointer="d251-a2"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
